<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#maharaja">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maharaja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maharajah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maharaja ('great king') is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> royal title used across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, originally for major sovereigns and later by many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the British the title was often granted or recognised by the paramount power as a mark of rank. The rulers' powers ended when the princely states joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> after 1947.</p></div>
